{
  "abstract": "Fix a nonperiodic odd unit $a$ (odd, prime to 3, and not on a Syracuse cycle). Its parents are the odd $x$ with $U(x)=a$, where $U(n)=(3n+1)/2^{v_2(3n+1)}$, and carry two laws: the natural law $Q_a(x)=d_x/d_a$ given by basin densities, and the canonical inverse law $P_{h,a}$ of Valbuena and Paper 12. Paper 102 proves power tails for the least ancestor $m_*(x)$ of the parent, and Papers 12 and 102 leave open whether the parent's own size has a power tail. Let $J_a(M)$ be the largest valuation $v_2(3x+1)$ of the last parent $x$ before the first hit on $a$, over odd $q\\le M$, $q\\ne a$, whose orbit reaches $a$. We prove that the parent size has a power tail under $Q_a$, or equivalently under $P_{h,a}$, if and only if $J_a(M)=O_a(\\log M)$, that is, if the least ancestor of every parent is at least a fixed power of the parent. Quantitatively, $J_a(M)\\le K\\log_2M+O_a(1)$ gives the tail exponent $\\eta/K$, with $\\eta$ from Paper 102, and a tail $CH^{-c}$ forces $J_a(M)\\le c^{-1}\\log_2M+O_a(1)$. So the question is pointwise, and density estimates cannot settle it alone. The same equivalence holds at a periodic root once its cycle parent is removed. At the root $1$, $2^{J_1(M)}$ is the largest first power of two on a $3n+1$ trajectory from an odd $q\\le M$. If the exponents of a source $q$ outside $r$ runs of ones sum to $s$, a $3$-adic Baker bound puts its first power of two below $3q\\cdot2^{O_{r,s}((\\log\\log q)^{2r})}$. Uniformly over roots, a power ceiling $d_x\\le Bx^{-c}$ on basin densities is equivalent, up to the exponents, to a polynomial bound on orbit heights. For $a=1,5,7,11,13$ and odd $q<2^{22}$, $J_a(M)-\\log_2(3M/a)$ lies in $[-1.9,3.9]$, consistent with $K=1$; at $a=1$ it is unbounded, of order at least $\\log\\log M$ along a sequence of $M$.",
